\section{Why a year-less object cannot simply be handed a code}{
Measured on the deployed \code{spatialize-country-grid} pin (58,795 cells, 178
area codes, no year): \strong{52,420 cells, 89.2\%, sit under an \code{area_code} that
\link{polity_area_crosswalk} maps to more than one polity over time}, so which
polity a cell belongs to is a function of the year the grid does not have.
The same holds for country labels resolved through \code{\link[=resolve_polity_label]{resolve_polity_label()}}:
33 of \link{mueller_synthetic_n}'s 156 \code{iso3c} values, 37 of \link{crops_manure_n}'s
184 \code{ISO} values and 38 of \code{gleam_geographic_hierarchy}'s 204 \code{iso3} values
name a \emph{different} polity at 1961 than at 2020.
}

\section{What counts as a territory here}{
A supra-national statistical grouping -- an IPCC or GLEAM region, a UN
sub-region, \code{region_krausmann} -- is \strong{not} a territory and is out of scope
by construction: it names a class of places, never a state, so it can never
acquire a polity code. Only the column names in
\code{whep:::.territory_key_names()} put an object in this register. That is also
why a bare \code{code} is not one of them: \code{biomass_coefs$Code} is a crop.
}

\section{The trap this register exists to make visible}{
\link{regions_full} and \link{polities_cats} carry a column literally named
\code{polity_code}, and \strong{none of its values is a \link{polities} code} -- it is a
legacy ISO3-like prefix kept for older callers, documented as such on both
datasets. Their real carrier is \code{reporting_polity_code}, a \link{polities} code
on all 259 of \code{regions_full}'s non-\code{NA} rows and all 198 of
\code{polities_cats}'s. Reading identity off the column whose name promises it
returns an empty join against every other polity-keyed table in WHEP.
}

